{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>If the flesh of the holy [portion]</b> — according to its plain sense, truly holy, and the opposite of יקדש יטמא. And if יקדש יטמא, why does he repeat, for this verse comes after that verse, and it is written: ולא תטמאו והייתם קדושים, and it [the offering] is sanctified from its impurity. And the word פן תקדש המלאה is holiness in truth, for everything becomes intermingled and reverts to holiness if they do not remove the holy [portion] in its proper time, as Menahem ben Saruq the Spaniard explained well. And the word קדשה — it sanctifies itself and readies itself for every desire; and the meaning of the word is not טמאה. Also the word קדש [is used] like the practice of the Egyptians regarding virgins, for the constitution of the Egyptians is weak because of the Nile, which increases their moisture; therefore they lack strength when they are near forty or older to deflower the virgin, until they bring a young man and he lies with her a little, until the entrance is opened — and this is called קדש, by way of prostitution, his craft being such. Therefore Scripture commanded: כמעשה ארץ מצרים וגו׳. Now the question concerns flesh of the holy [portion], concerning which it is written וכל אשר יגע בבשרה יקדש: if the vessel — that is, the wing [fold of the garment] — in which the flesh is placed, that is, the holy [portion], touches the bread, or one of the aforementioned things — will it become holy? Then the priests answered and said: No. And their word was correct, since the holy [portion] did not touch any of these — only the wing touched them, and the flesh of the holy [portion] did not touch them in fact — and by the same reasoning, they did not touch the flesh of the holy [portion]."], :book :haggai, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The comment weaves together several distinct scriptural discussions (Leviticus prohibitions, Egyptian customs referenced in Leviticus 18:3, and the Haggai passage); translated as a continuous unit preserving the compressed flow. The phrase והיא מתקדשת מטומאתה is rendered hyper-literally; the referent of והיא is ambiguous."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Haggai" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/hagai"] ["Ibn Ezra on Haggai -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/hagai"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:49:38.432258022Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}